{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785594262,"nonce":"0fF5NTz83CtzbDw+SNhvF1hfAts=","title":"From the 212bb13 stage-1 review (m-2, m-3, m-5, m-6 — the in-Pod drain's resource story, bundled):","message":"1. The egress container has NO resource requests/limits, and drain memory moved into the Pod: scan reads each file wholly into RAM and the upload stage re-reads at CONCURRENCY=32 → peak ≈ 32 × largest blob. An OOM-kill classifies Transient and loops. Decide requests/limits (values knob?) and whether scan/upload should stream large blobs instead of whole-file reads.\n2. A FIFO/device node in /workspace blocks std::fs::read forever; no timeout exists anywhere in the drive path, so the escalation clock never fires (pre-existing hazard class, new hang site). The scan should refuse or skip-with-error non-regular files explicitly.\n3. Workspace token expiry = step timeout + 600s capped at 24h — a step with timeout \u003e= 24h drains with an expired token → 401 → exit 10 → transient loop. Document the constraint or mint the drain leg a fresh token.\n4. ledger_append never dedupes: every re-drain re-appends the full closure; ledgers/ + drains/ also count into the warm-size gauge. Dedupe on append (or compact on sweep) and exclude bookkeeping dirs from the gauge (folds git-bug 1a9df08's gauge-honesty thread).\n- [ ] Each of the four has either a fix or an explicit accepted-and-documented verdict","files":null}]}